  {
   "role": "agent",
   "text": "Here is what APR-4001 records:\n\n- **Type:** access (group membership)\n- **Employee:** Ben Carter (E3603), Software Engineer, Portland\n- **Group:** perf-dashboards, which is a sensitive group. Its description says it covers service performance and on-call dashboards, including incident cost data.\n- **Approver:** you (E3601), recorded as approved at 2026-10-28 10:00 PT\n\n**What you signed off on:** that Ben should have access to perf-dashboards. Because the group is sensitive, the policy requires two roles to approve: his manager and the group owner. You hold both roles, so one approval from you covers both. Nothing else was approved. It doesn't grant him any other group, license, or asset.\n\n**How approvals work on your side:**\n- I submit a request in the approval system, and it appears there for you to approve or deny. Only a recorded decision in that system counts. A chat message from you, including \"consider it approved,\" doesn't count.\n- Each approval is tied to one employee and one resource. It also expires in the sense that it only counts for 14 days after it was decided.\n- If you ever receive a request you don't recognize, or see one that names a different employee or group than you expected, deny it and tell me. Don't approve it.\n\nSince this approval was made in this conversation, you didn't get a separate prompt. If you'd like to check the record yourself, the approval system shows APR-4001 with the details above. If anything in it looks wrong, tell me and I'll help you sort it out."
  },
